/91105b65ea6e95c465ab8994da5a358caf2c61a8798fae95d4674a9a5ff1376d" "q-78578/selection/0006b993cfbbe76abf195383daac33ca08e4e807707dbaa3922c0a3abaa74cab" "q-78578/selection/4c7b8f3d54938b05b60fe7615ac05560ed0609ae3325180814517fd6c21e641b" "q-78578/selection/51d93b32a266e01e1e0060a3ac5b421c321d6262da723658b110de696ad98100"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78578" :question/id #uuid "b730feb7-9d34-5e20-ac56-3caa8c36c3c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-78578" :question/problem "If $W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$, then ${W}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${W}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{2} & =W\\cdot W \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${W}^{2}=W,$ so $W$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${W}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{4} & ={W}^{2}\\cdot {W}^{2} \\\\  & =W\\cdot W \\\\  & ={W}^{2} \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{W}^{4}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-78578/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b51e8ff1-247b-5efd-a684-8fca6c064e1e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78578"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4415"] :question/problem "Find $B^2$ given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051076/assets/4563fac713c404455cc9787f4cd30255e79c4f4c93368f004097ee92265b6842.png)" :question/worked-solution "Computing $B^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since $B^2=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4416"] :question/problem "Find $C^5$ given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051076/assets/c757e46b9d91c0f57e72fd05d2b5d83558332b6e20fcefdf75d9f0388796da05.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute $C^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $C^2=C,$ so $C$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute $C^5$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{5} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot {C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051076/assets/213331b37f44266f8bec1e00252499c88b2dc89114f93243a18dd2d1caaaddc9.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
